/* CalendarDialog embeds a uise--Calendar (which already draws its own 6px padding, see
 * calendar.qss) directly as the dialog's content. Without this override
 * uise--AbstractDialog #contentFrame's own "padding: 16px 8px 8px 8px" (abstractdialog.qss)
 * would stack on top of it, giving the grid a lopsided, oversized margin -- CalendarDropdown
 * gets the tighter look this reproduces by having only its own 6px padding, nothing else. */
uise--CalendarDialog #contentFrame {padding: 0;}

/* CalendarDropdown gives its own equivalent wrapper (#calendarDropdownContent, calendar.qss)
 * an explicit transparent background for the same reason: once a widget acquires
 * WA_StyledBackground -- which #calendarDialogContent does here, purely by having its own
 * objectName-qualified rules like the one above -- Qt Style Sheets stop treating it as the
 * ordinarily-transparent plain QFrame it would otherwise be, and fill it from the active
 * palette instead. Left unset, that shows up as a mismatched grey box behind Calendar's own
 * (truly transparent, see calendar.qss) header title label, most visible in the dark theme. */
uise--CalendarDialog #calendarDialogContent {background-color: transparent;}

/* Same spacing as CalendarDropdown's own buttons row (calendar.qss) -- the Apply-styled button
 * below the calendar is built the same way as CalendarDropdown's, so it gets the same margin
 * above it. */
uise--CalendarDialog #buttonsFrame {margin-top: 4px;}

/* The Apply button is a bare checkmark, in every state. Without these rules the inner QPushButton is
 * left to the platform style in some states (a disabled one is drawn as a native bezel on macOS: a
 * grey bordered box around a faint tick). The disabled look is the lighter tick alone. */
uise--CalendarDialog #applyButton QPushButton,
uise--CalendarDialog #applyButton QPushButton:hover,
uise--CalendarDialog #applyButton QPushButton:disabled {
border: none;
background-color: transparent;
}
